        },
        {
            "speaker": "Assistant",
            "message": "And does it also work with more vectors?",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "Continue"
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "Yes! You can sum any number of vectors and get a new vector w.",
            "contentType": "Image",
            "interaction": "Continue",
            "imageData": {
                "imageName": "vectorPath.png"
            }
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "And what about this combination? How do I describe it mathematically?",
            "contentType": "Image",
            "interaction": "MultipleChoice",
            "imageData": {
                "imageName": "2u-v.png"
            },
            "multipleChoice": {
                "answers": [
                    {
                        "text": "2u + v",
                        "isCorrect": false,
                        "feedback": "No, no, the vector −v is the opposite of the vector v."
                    },
                    {
                        "text": "2u - v",
                        "isCorrect": true,
                        "feedback": "Well done, the vector −v is the opposite of the vector v.",
                        "action": {
                            "actionType": "Image",
                            "imageData": {
                                "imageName": "2u-v-annotated.png"
                            }
                        }
                    },
                    {
                        "text": "u + 2v",
                        "isCorrect": false,
                        "feedback": "Check again — here it’s the vector v that is doubled, not u."
                    },
                    {
                        "text": "u - 2v",
                        "isCorrect": false,
                        "feedback": "Check again — here it’s the vector v that is doubled, not u."
                    }
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Assistant",
            "message": "Hmmm, can any 2D vector be written as a linear combination of \nu = (2, 1) and v = (-1, 3)?",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "MultipleChoice",
            "multipleChoice": {
                "answers": [
                    {
                        "text": "Yes",
                        "isCorrect": true,
                        "feedback": "Yes, u and v indeed span the entire 2D plane."
                    },
                    {
                        "text": "No",
                        "isCorrect": false
                    }
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "Formally we can say {αu+βv ∣ α,β ∈ ℝ} = ℝ² for vectors u = (2, 1) and v = (-1, 3).",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "Continue"
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "For example, what linear combination of u and v gives the vector (0, 7)?",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "MultipleChoice",
            "multipleChoice": {
                "answers": [
                    {
                        "text": "α=3, β=-2",
                        "isCorrect": false,
                        "feedback": "3*(2, 1) - 2*(-1, 3) = (8, -3), not (0, 7)."
                    },
                    {
                        "text": "α=1, β=-2",
                        "isCorrect": false,
                        "feedback": "1*(2, 1) - 2*(-1, 3) = (4, -5), not (0, 7)."
                    },
                    {
                        "text": "α=1, β=2",
                        "isCorrect": true,
                        "feedback": "Exactly, because 1*(2, 1) + 2(-1, 3) = (0, 7)."
                    }
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Assistant",
            "message": "Well, that’s easy! And is there a word for all the vectors I can get through linear combination?",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "Continue"
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "Yes, we call it Linear Span or Linear Hull (lineární obal in Czech).",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "Continue",
            "saveFact": "true",
            "factData": {
                "text1": "The span (linear hull) of vectors u and v",
                "text2": "A set of all their linear combinations: Span(u,v) = { αu + βv ∣ α, β ∈ ℝ }",
                "speaker": "Teacher"
            }
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "Let’s review this lesson with pair matching.",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "Pairing",
            "pairs": [
                {
                    "id": 0,
                    "left": "Result of a linear combination?",
